+`omarchy-echo-cancel.service` (a user unit, enabled for every user, run only where `/proc/asound/cards` lists a microphone array card, `AppleJNNNHPAI` or the J700's `AppleJ700AOP`) cancels the speakers' echo in the built-in microphones, which callers otherwise hear: asahi-audio's speaker graph (bass synthesis, compressors, limiters) changes the sound after an app plays it, so an app's own canceller (WebRTC's, in browsers and Electron apps) leaves the echo in. It runs `pipewire -c /usr/share/omarchy-mac/echo-cancel.conf`, PipeWire's WebRTC echo canceller in monitor mode: the reference is the default output's monitor (`omarchy_echo_cancel.reference`), the input is the mapping (`omarchy_echo_cancel.capture`, which never falls back to another source), and the result is `omarchy_echo_cancel.source` ("MacBook Microphone"), with gain control off and noise suppression on. Its `priority.session` of 1 keeps WirePlumber from choosing it; the mapper selects it in place of the mapping, with the same signal and route checks, on a first mapping and once on an install whose default input is already the mapping (`omarchy/asahi-mic-gain.echo-cancel` under XDG_STATE_HOME records that move, so a mapping chosen later stays). It is part of `pipewire.service`, so it restarts with PipeWire. On a MacBook Pro 16" M4 Max (J616c) at loud speaker volume, the echo reached the microphones 14 to 16 dB below what was played, about 100 ms later; the canceller removed 42 to 55 dB of it.
